  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Is it true that if $A\\subset \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a set of $n$ points such that every subset of $3$ points determines $3$ distinct distances (i.e. $A$ has no isosceles triangles) then $A$ must determine at least $f(n)n$ distinct distances, for some $f(n)\\to \\infty$?",
